WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.784 --> 00:00:12.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.110
FENG LU: Hi, welcome
to Cloud on Air--

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.860
Webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.260
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.180
My name is Feng Lu, and I'm a
software engineer at Google.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.540
Today I will be talking
about Cloud Composer.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.020
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.474
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:31.474 --> 00:00:32.390
Now let's get started.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.750
So today I plan to cover
the following topics.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.365
I will start by giving
an overview of Composer.

00:00:41.365 --> 00:00:42.740
Next, we'll talk
about how to use

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.140
Composer, followed by a demo.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.180
And I will wrap up
my talk with pointers

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.170
on how to get you started.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.610
So before we start, like,
what's Cloud Composer?

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.980
What kind of problems are
we trying to solve here?

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:03.090
In many cases, we find a need
to run something, run a task

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:06.900
or run certain operations,
at a predefined interval,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.860
or in response to
an actual input.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.370
Now, we not only find
that in our workplace.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.990
Sometimes it happens
in your life as well.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.200
It's just the case that
you don't realize that.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.960
For example, you may have
an alarm that wakes you

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.710
up every day at
8:00 AM, or you may

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.130
want to send an email
every Thursday night.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.760
So for a task as
simple as that, Cron,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.500
which is a utility in
Unix operating system,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.630
might be sufficient
for your need.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.520
Sometimes when your task gets
a little bit complicated--

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.970
for example, you may
have a few tasks,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.760
and there are some
dependencies among those tasks.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.330
In this slide, I have an
example in the middle.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:55.770
Imagine that you have Google
App Engine application, which

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:00.480
is constantly dumping logs
into the Google Cloud Storage

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.470
bucket.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.420
And every once in
a while, you want

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.320
to run a Dataflow job that
processes and cleans up those

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.870
logs and saves
them to BigQuery so

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:16.230
that you can run useful
analytics from the user data.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.830
Even a task where--

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.470
a workflow as simple
as what I just

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.590
described, if you think about
how you want to implement that,

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.750
it's actually a lot of work.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.780
You need to make sure you
understand the APIs that talk

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.140
to the various Cloud services.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:36.930
You need to make sure you
have the right authorization

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.959
and account in
place, and not even

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:42.000
to mention that you have
to worry about failures.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.210
There could be service
failures, network failures.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.970
So at the end of
day, you realize

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.790
that you spend a lot of
time not actually focusing

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.630
on your workflow, but rather
on all the infrastructures

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.940
that run your workflow.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.740
I probably would
not even attempt

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.970
to try to use Cron to run
a sharded machine learning

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:03.620
pipeline.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.010
That's just too complicated.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:12.360
So we believe that it should
be easy to create, schedule,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.100
and monitor workflows that work
inside and outside of Google

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:17.840
Cloud Platform.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.660
With that mission in mind,
we introduce Cloud Composer

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:24.610
service.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:29.000
In a nutshell, Cloud Composer is
really a managed Apache Airflow

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.050
service that makes workflow
creation, management

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.700
powerful, consistent,
and easy for you.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.510
We start the Cloud
Composer project

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.540
with a number of
product goals in mind.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.720
We wanted to have a
comprehensive integration

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.550
with a lot of Cloud services.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:53.480
So like I mentioned earlier, as
a workflow author or a workflow

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.020
user, you don't have
to worry about talking

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.400
to the various Cloud services.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.190
So we do want to provide
high-level abstractions so

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.600
that it make it easier for
you to start using other Cloud

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.520
services.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:13.100
We also don't want to limit your
workflow to just be task-only.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:19.610
We want it to be the case that
it's just as easy as for you

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:22.160
to start jobs
elsewhere-- for example,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.950
in another cloud provider,
or even on Prime--

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.470
as starting a job in
Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.440
We want to make the
workflow specification,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.715
or the definition of the
workflow, easier for you

00:04:34.715 --> 00:04:35.480
as well.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.620
So hence we choose
the popular and easy

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.050
to use language Python for
you to write your workflow

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:43.400
definition.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.010
And finally, we want
to have open-source

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:51.710
in the core of Cloud Composer
so that your workflow is

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.080
agnostic to where
it gets executed.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:59.030
You can transparently move your
workflow from GCP to on-prem,

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.030
to another cloud provider.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:06.400
So next, I'm going to talk
about some of the key features

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.800
as well as some of the
design decisions we

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.300
have made to fulfill the product
goals I just articulated.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.210
We really try to--
so in Composer, we

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.370
provide a list of key features.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.860
And those key
features are centered

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:23.260
around three main schemes--

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:27.700
simplicity, security, and
production monitoring.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.540
I will explain each one in turn.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:34.860
So for simplicity, we
have convenient tools

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.770
like Cloud SDK or
Cloud Console for you

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.390
to interact and manage
Composer services.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:48.880
We also make it easy for you
to manage your workflow DAGs.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.970
We make it easy for
you to manage plug-ins.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:58.100
And then, moving on
to the security bit,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:02.360
we use Cloud IAM to manage all
the credentials, permissions,

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:04.320
and access policies.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.400
Composer also comes together
with built-in support

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.640
for Cloud
Identity-Aware Proxy so

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.690
that your workflow-monitoring
[INAUDIBLE]

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:17.210
web server is secured
and protected.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.970
And finally, we realize the--

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.670
we also integrate
with Stackdriver

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:26.390
to provide you with logging
and monitoring metrics

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:27.800
so that you can
build a dashboard

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.570
to visualize the internal
state of with your workflows

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:35.760
and also alert you when your
workflow is not running well.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.465
I'm going to discuss a key
concept in Cloud Composer

00:06:44.465 --> 00:06:46.400
that's called environment.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.520
So what is a
Composer environment?

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.760
It's nothing more than
just a managed deployment

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.560
of Apache Airflow.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.320
Inside a single GSW
project, you could

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:57.920
have multiple environments.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.430
And those environments
are perfectly isolated

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.910
from each other.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:06.790
In each environment, we
provide you with Cloud storage,

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:09.640
where you can manage your
DAG sources or your workflow

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:10.840
sources.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.180
And you also have the stack.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:19.160
We also provide you individual
workflow web servers

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.970
for you to access your
workflow status pertaining

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:23.990
to their specific environment.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.630
Finally, we have
Stackdriver, that

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:28.690
provides convenient
tools for you

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:31.949
to navigate through
multiple environments.

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:33.490
And the last thing
I want to mention,

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:37.990
that each environment is
protected by Cloud IAM.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.020
So you can config
permissions individually

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.160
for each environment.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.990
Now we're going to talk about
some of the design decisions.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:50.860
Earlier, I mentioned
that Composer

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.470
has open-source in its core.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.650
And you know, there are a lot
of open-source orchestration

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:57.980
solutions.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.090
Why did we decide
on Apache Airflow?

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:02.530
For those of you
who are not very

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.660
familiar with Apache Airflow,
here's a quick history on it.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.660
It was originally from Airbnb.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:15.490
And that project has donated
to Apache Software Foundation.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:21.140
It's in incubation stage now,
but it's soon to graduate.

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:25.820
We chose Apache Airflow
for a number of benefits.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:27.670
Number one, it's open-source.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:30.040
It's based on
Python, easy to use.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.409
It has a lot of [INAUDIBLE]
interface today.

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:34.120
You know, you have web server.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:38.809
It has a very nice web server,
convenient command-line tools.

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:41.049
It also has an experimental
API server for you

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:44.870
to interact with the
service programmatically.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.010
One thing I want to
highlight for Apache Airflow

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:51.330
is that it has a very powerful
and flexible scheduling

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.190
and dependency mechanisms.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.140
So pretty much, you
can encode a lot

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.990
of different dependency
relationships among your tasks

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:01.920
in a workflow.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.270
It has a lot of
community-contributed

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:05.800
connectors.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:10.300
For example, you have
connectors to AWS, Google Cloud

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:14.660
Platform, interfaces to a
lot of databases as well.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:17.770
And last thing is the
Apache Airflow community

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:20.230
is very active.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:25.080
There are lots of
commits, many developers.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.600
Overall, it's a fairly
active community.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:31.270
On the downside,
I think, probably,

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:36.310
when the project got started, it
wasn't really focused a lot on,

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:40.320
for example, a production setup,
permission management, logging,

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.180
debugging, and all that stuff.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:45.940
For example, in
Apache Airflow, we

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:47.770
don't really
mandate, say, how you

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:53.620
want to run your web server, how
would you scale your machines

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.930
when the workload gets more.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.960
So those happen to be the things
that Google Cloud Platform is

00:09:58.960 --> 00:09:59.620
good at.

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:05.860
So in Cloud Composer, we provide
infrastructure scalability.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:12.070
We have security built in with
Cloud IAP as well as Cloud IAM.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.760
We make it really easy
for you to deploy DAGs.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:18.730
And we have Stackdriver
login and monitoring for you

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:23.950
to make sure your Airflow
environment's running well.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:27.820
I also wanted to mention,
another point is that we

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:29.530
don't--

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:33.640
Google doesn't intend to just
fork a copy of Apache Airflow

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.890
and just make a
service ourselves.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:38.140
We want to work together
with the community,

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:41.480
and together, enhance and
improve Apache Airflow.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:44.080
So in the past, we
have worked together

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:48.100
with the community to contribute
the Kubernetes executor back

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:49.837
to the Airflow community.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:58.240
As I mentioned,
Composer/Airflow comes together

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.570
with a number of
built-in services.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:04.730
I'm not going to read
out all the services.

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:10.080
Some of the notable
ones are BigQuery, GCS,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.160
[? pop-ups, ?] data flow.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:16.080
So as a Composer user, you
don't have to worry about,

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.510
how do I interact
with those services?

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:25.510
Instead, Composer or Airflow
provides nice operators for you

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:28.210
to start interacting
with those services

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:31.140
without worrying about
authentication and API details.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:38.360
I'm also going to give you
a little bit of details

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:43.130
on architectures, how
we build Composer.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:46.550
So if you look at the
overview of the architecture,

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:49.190
it has two projects.

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:50.660
One is the customer project.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.000
And the other is
the tenant project.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.010
So customer project
is really the things

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:56.680
you see on a daily
basis when you're

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:00.170
logged into your Cloud console,
where you use [INAUDIBLE] SDK.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:02.360
So those other
projects owned by you.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:06.350
And you have full admin
control over those projects.

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:09.290
On the other side,
there's tenant project.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:13.880
Tenant project, again, is also
a Google Cloud Platform project.

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.130
But those projects are
owned by the service.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:19.850
In this case, it's the
Cloud Composer service.

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:23.960
We decided to host some
services in tenant project

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:28.240
for enhanced security
and better isolation.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:31.840
We'll come to that
point in a minute.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:39.120
In this setup, we
tried to set up

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:44.640
in a way that sort of reflects
the popular Airflow Plus

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:47.070
[? pseudo ?] executor config.

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:51.660
Given the microservice nature
of the framework of Airflow,

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:55.590
our implementation also sort
of reflects that structure.

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:58.290
For example, there will be
a Airflow scheduler service.

00:12:58.290 --> 00:12:59.700
There's an Airflow worker.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.710
There's also an
Airflow web server.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:04.770
So Airflow scheduler
and Airflow worker,

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:09.870
we host both inside
Kubernetes engines

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:11.670
in the customer project.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.280
And then for Airflow web
server, as I mentioned,

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:16.650
we hosted that web
server in tenant project

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:20.670
so we can have integrated and
built-in support for cloud IAP

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:22.140
protection.

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.230
Instead of configuring
IAP yourself,

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:26.820
Cloud Composer does
that it for you.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:29.580
And you manage that
access to your web server

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:32.280
using Composer permissions.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:33.900
In addition, you
probably have also

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:38.070
noticed that we have put
the Airflow database inside

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:40.330
of tenant project as well.

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:46.080
The reason behind it is that
we believe since Airflow stores

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:49.890
connection, which is the
access credentials in Airflow

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:51.370
database.

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:53.340
So we want to make sure
that that database is

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:57.710
only accessible to a
very narrow set of users

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.560
with service accounts.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:03.780
In our default setup,
the Airflow database

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:06.180
is only accessible to
the service account

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:10.050
that you used to run
the Kubernetes engine.

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:14.280
By the way, during
Composer creation,

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.680
you have the choice
to specify which

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:21.290
service account you want to use
to run your Kubernetes engine.

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:24.050
So let's move back to
the customer project.

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:27.710
We have [INAUDIBLE],, which
is the [INAUDIBLE] backend

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:29.840
for messaging distribution.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:34.560
And we have the SQL proxy
for accessing the database.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:37.520
Moving down the graph a
little bit, we see that DAGs,

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.920
plugins, and logs.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:43.910
Those folders are all
created inside per project,

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:49.320
per environment, Google
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:52.590
Again, Composer manages the
distribution of the DAGs

00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:54.840
to your various
Airflow services so you

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.890
don't have to worry
about how do you

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:00.600
make sure your Airflow
service is going

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:04.350
to sync to the latest stacks--

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:07.230
likewise for plugins.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:09.420
In Airflow, the
plugins are for you

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:12.070
to write customized operators.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:15.390
Here, Composer would also take
care of plugin integration

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:18.310
into Airflow services.

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:22.530
And finally, at the
left side of bottom,

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:26.400
you see the logs, the
Stackdriver Logging.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.850
In addition to Airflow logs,
the scheduler and worker

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:32.940
logs also saved in Stackdriver.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:36.420
We do that so that it provides
a centralized place for you

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.330
to navigate through all
the logs, but in addition,

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:44.110
also try to make streaming logs
really fast and really on time

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:44.610
for you.

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:50.506
So here's a quick recap.

00:15:50.506 --> 00:15:52.130
We have just talked
about what Composer

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:55.490
is, the problems
Composer tries to solve,

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:59.330
some of our initial
product goals,

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:02.450
some of the Composer
notable features,

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.460
and our design decisions.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:06.290
Next, we're going to
talk about how can we

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:10.340
use Composer to actually
author and schedule a workflow?

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:17.860
So to create and access
Composer environments,

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:19.020
there are three ways.

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:22.950
You could use Cloud SDK, which
is our command line tool.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:27.010
Or you can use the cloud
console, which is the UI tool.

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:29.220
And finally, you can also
interact with the service

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:31.035
programatically using our API.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:36.750
There is no
additional setup step

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:46.290
you need to do when start
to work on workflows.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.740
Once the composing
environment is created,

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:51.375
the next thing you
need to do is to focus

00:16:51.375 --> 00:16:54.540
on your workflow developments.

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:56.890
In the context of
Apache Airflow,

00:16:56.890 --> 00:17:00.630
it's also called DAG, which
stands for Direct Acyclic

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:01.170
Graph.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.520
What that means is
in your workflow,

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:06.849
you shouldn't
really have cycles.

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:11.040
So here's a quick example about
how typical workflow with DAG

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.510
would look like.

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:18.089
Imagine that you have some files
that is hosted on-prem in HDFS.

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:21.329
And then periodically, you
want to copy that to file--

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:23.640
say for example, if you
detect the new files

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:28.319
and then you want to copy
that files to GCS bucket.

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:30.420
And once the data
is available in GCS,

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:32.730
you may want to
launch a BigQuery job

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:35.550
and then try to process
the data and then

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:39.460
get some useful
insights from the data.

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:41.800
And once the insights
are available,

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:46.740
you may want to send out
that data to a Slack channel.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:48.900
So that could be some
of the typical workflow

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.200
you will run inside Composer.

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:56.910
There are about five steps
really for you to write

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.110
an Airflow DAG or workflow.

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:03.160
We summarize that
into five steps--

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:07.260
import, write arguments,
do your DAG setup,

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:10.890
lay down the various tasks, and
then encode task dependencies.

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:15.710
So this is the standard part.

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:18.140
Before you write
your DAG or workflow,

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.450
you import all of the
necessary libraries.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:24.060
In this case, you want to make
sure you important the DAGs,

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:26.390
the operators from Airflow.

00:18:26.390 --> 00:18:29.640
And if you have specific or
customized trigger rules,

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.920
you also want to import
that from Airflow as well.

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:35.210
And once you have
that import in place,

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:37.640
you could start to
construct your arguments.

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:42.230
So Airflow DAG has a default
DAG arguments section,

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:45.080
which for you to specify
[INAUDIBLE] arguments that

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:49.010
apply to all tasks in the DAG.

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:51.750
So for example, you can specify
when you want your DAG to get

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:54.380
started, whether you
want to receive email

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:58.790
when there's a DAG failure, and
if a task fails, how many times

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:00.710
you want to retry that task.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:04.250
What's the delay between
consecutive retries?

00:19:04.250 --> 00:19:11.100
So this ties back to our earlier
discussion on writing workflows

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:12.110
yourself.

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:15.260
I mean, in this Cron
plus scripting example,

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:18.320
you have to worry all
those retries yourself.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:22.594
And you have to actually built
in your own email operator

00:19:22.594 --> 00:19:24.635
inside so that you make
sure you get notification

00:19:24.635 --> 00:19:27.560
when a DAG or a workflow fails.

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:30.440
Here, in Composer
or in Airflow, you

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:33.380
get all that being
provided for you already.

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:36.380
The only thing you need to do
is write your configuration

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:40.080
and leave all the details
to Composer or Airflow.

00:19:42.670 --> 00:19:45.550
So after that, if you have
additional global variables

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:47.890
that you plan to use
in your DAG, again,

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:49.000
you can list them here.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:53.350
So for example, here we
have the GitHub table.

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:56.665
We have the output file
and the BQ dataset names.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:05.130
After that, you start
to set up your DAG

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:09.240
by giving the DAG a
name, define an interval

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:12.434
that how often a DAG should
be triggered and executed.

00:20:12.434 --> 00:20:14.100
And then of course,
you past the default

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:16.920
args, arguments to the DAG.

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:18.840
There's one thing I
want to highlight here,

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:22.260
is that make sure that your
schedule interval is set here.

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:25.210
You can't set a schedule
interval in a default args.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:26.499
It won't work there.

00:20:26.499 --> 00:20:28.290
So that's the one thing
I want to point out

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:29.340
when you write your DAGs.

00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:34.600
The next step is basically
to lay down all the tasks

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.770
you need in this workflow.

00:20:36.770 --> 00:20:40.390
So in this specific
example, we have two tasks.

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:42.570
One is about running
out Bigquery task.

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:46.360
The other one is publishing
a Bigquery output to GCS.

00:20:46.360 --> 00:20:50.620
As you can imagine, you can
have as many tasks as you want.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.350
Airflow also allows you to
programmatically generate

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:54.450
a task--

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:58.270
for example, write a
user for loop to generate

00:20:58.270 --> 00:20:59.360
and array of tasks.

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:01.840
So all this is entirely
possible in Airflow.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:08.990
And finally, you specify
the dependency or orderings

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:10.490
among your task.

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:12.800
In this case, what
this line says,

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:16.010
I want to run my
BigQuery first before I

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:19.694
expose the result
to GCS, which makes

00:21:19.694 --> 00:21:20.860
a lot of sense in this case.

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:25.520
Once you have everything
together, then

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:26.730
you're good to go.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:30.140
So now we're going to give a
[INAUDIBLE] demo using Composer

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:33.500
so that we tie all the
concepts we have discussed.

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:50.970
Now back to the demo.

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:53.540
I, for the sake of
the presentation,

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:56.950
try to save some time,
I have pre-created

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:59.730
a composing environment.

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:04.640
So if you click on one of the
environments, I call it like,

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:08.046
in this case, it's
called a composer demo-.

00:22:08.046 --> 00:22:09.950
It has a number of tabs.

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:12.140
And then you got to see
all the details that

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:15.680
pertain to a specified
composing environment.

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:18.380
I'm going to quickly go
over some of the properties.

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:22.280
Don't worry if you can read
all the text on the screen.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.170
For example, you have
an environment name.

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:26.620
You have a location,
whether this environment

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:29.510
is running in US central,
Europe, or West one.

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:32.510
You've got a chance to
specify service account.

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:35.960
There's also a specific
Composer version

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:39.440
that you are running
in this environment--

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:42.770
now some details related
to the worker node,

00:22:42.770 --> 00:22:46.490
which we use to run Airflow
scheduler and Airflow workers--

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:49.690
[INAUDIBLE] machine type,
the disk size, and et cetera.

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:55.380
There are two things that you
want to pay special attention.

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.230
One is called DAGs folder.

00:22:57.230 --> 00:23:02.480
So if we click through
that DAGs folder,

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:08.060
that leads you to a place where
all DAGs should be staged.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:10.670
To deploy a DAG is as
simple as I could drop

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:13.700
a file in this DAGs folder.

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:16.340
And likewise, you realize that
there's another one that's

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:18.140
called Airflow Web UI.

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:20.480
If you click that web
UI, that leads you

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:24.230
to the familiar Airflow
web UI interface.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:30.770
You would be able to access all
workflow-related information as

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:32.840
well as some of
the configuration

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:34.100
and the admin panel.

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:39.970
In order to access this
Airflow web server,

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:42.580
you need to make sure that
you have the right permission.

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:45.120
So I skipped the
part [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:47.950
[? login, ?] it probably
won't prompt me for login.

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:50.140
But if you are going
to create one yourself

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:53.380
and try it offline, you
probably will see a login window

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:56.750
prompting you for
email and password.

00:23:56.750 --> 00:24:01.360
So that's the way
how CLOUD IAP works.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.150
You check that the user
has a write permission

00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:09.540
before it allows you to
open the Airflow web UI.

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:12.940
So as I mentioned,
deploying a DAG,

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:14.960
it's as simple as
dropping a file here.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:17.410
So let me get that started.

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:20.050
So I'm going to
upload a DAG file.

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:23.410
This DAG file, in fact,
it's called Quick Start,

00:24:23.410 --> 00:24:26.950
is, in fact, the Quick
Start guide in our cloud

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:30.180
documentation website.

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:33.875
So I just upload this one.

00:24:33.875 --> 00:24:35.600
It's done.

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:40.120
It takes a few minutes for
Airflow to pick up the DAG

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:41.590
and to start execution.

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:45.590
While waiting for Airflow to
pick up the DAGs, and then I

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:48.274
also want to give you some
quick overview on some

00:24:48.274 --> 00:24:49.690
of the other
configurations you're

00:24:49.690 --> 00:24:53.440
going to be able to
do in Cloud Composer.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:58.220
We allowed you to update Airflow
configuration in real time.

00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:02.330
So for example, if you want to
hide the configuration admin

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:06.650
panel, you could just make a
change to say, hey, web server,

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:10.600
expose configure to
[? force. ?] After that change,

00:25:10.600 --> 00:25:16.400
your configuration will be
hidden from the login users.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:20.500
We also allowed you to
install PyPI dependencies.

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:22.990
Imagine that your
workflow requires, say,

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:27.880
select client to be installed
or [INAUDIBLE] to be installed.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:31.890
You could simply just use
the PyPI package management

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:35.620
feature in Composer and
then specify the0 packages

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:37.400
you want to install.

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:40.870
So for example, you
could add a package.

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:44.420
You could add a slackclient.

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:47.012
And then you can also specify
option of which [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:47.012 --> 00:25:48.220
So that's all you need to do.

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:49.700
After that, you click Submit.

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:52.420
That's all you need to do to
get to the dependency installed

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:53.700
for you.

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:57.710
Now, let's come back
to the Airflow web UI.

00:25:57.710 --> 00:26:00.540
Let's do a refresh, because
Airflow web server doesn't

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:03.730
do refresh by itself.

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:04.240
Now we go.

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:11.400
We see the Quick Start
start to appear in a web UI.

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:13.320
Because, as we
mentioned earlier,

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:15.460
Airflow is on
microservice architecture,

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:18.000
so you have a web
server component.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:20.060
It also has a scheduler
component. [? Notes ?]

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:22.390
component are not
perfectly synced.

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:28.080
So sometimes if you see things
like DAGs being recognized

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:30.330
in one place and
not the other place,

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:33.030
just gave it a few
more minutes or just do

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:34.860
a few more refreshes.

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:37.320
And hopefully, you'll see
the DAGs being shown soon.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:38.740
There we go.

00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:42.880
So click through the DAGs.

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:47.410
It has different views for you
to navigate through your DAG

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:48.090
structure.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:50.550
There's a graph
view so that you see

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:54.730
that we have three tasks, like
create a dataproc cluster,

00:26:54.730 --> 00:26:56.490
run a simple
[? work con ?] example,

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:59.010
and the delete the
dataproc cluster.

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:04.440
There's different views,
like graph view, tree view.

00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:07.360
And you will also be
able to see the details--

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:10.050
for example, how long does
it take for a particular task

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:12.240
to complete?

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:14.666
In many cases, if you
want to inspect the code,

00:27:14.666 --> 00:27:16.040
you could also
see the code here.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:22.040
So let's go back to the web UI.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:24.560
Right now, we are
in a stage of trying

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:27.710
to create a dataproc cluster.

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:29.900
So we can go to
view the logs, see

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:32.300
what has been specified there.

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:34.860
This is essentially says,
hey, right now this task

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:35.360
has started.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:40.730
Some of you may wonder,
given that Airflow already

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:45.020
has workflow or task
logs, why you still

00:27:45.020 --> 00:27:49.570
want to provide work logs
or logs in Stackdriver?

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:51.020
Here's one difference.

00:27:51.020 --> 00:28:04.150
If you go to Stackdriver
Logging, go to Composer demo,

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:09.450
then go to Airflow
workers, you actually

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:11.310
see a lot of more things here.

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:16.340
The reason between [? site ?]
differently, Airflow tries

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:18.960
to upload logs periodically.

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:24.000
So it comes together where
depends on some internal cause.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:26.310
So you don't always
see the latest logs.

00:28:26.310 --> 00:28:28.750
But with Stackdriver
Logging in Composer,

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:31.980
you would be able to see all the
logs that happen in real time.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:35.610
Sometimes if you happen to
have a task that takes a while

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:39.420
to complete-- a few minutes
or in some extreme case,

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:41.220
maybe an hour to finish--

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:44.070
and [? this, ?] you want to be
able to see those logs earlier.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:47.520
So Stackdriver logs would
be the place where you could

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:48.920
see all the streaming logs.

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:53.660
Let's go back.

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:58.400
As we can see, the created
dataproc cluster task is done.

00:28:58.400 --> 00:29:02.540
Now we move to the
work [? cons ?] task.

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:04.580
One thing you
notice that Airflow

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:08.150
works as a UI to highlight the
different stage of the task.

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:11.810
For example, you get a dark
green when a task is done,

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:15.334
relatively light green when
a task is still active.

00:29:15.334 --> 00:29:16.750
I will give you a
few more minutes

00:29:16.750 --> 00:29:18.630
to let the workflow to complete.

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:22.030
But as you can see,
once the task is done,

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:24.980
you will to be able to see a
lot more details in the log.

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:28.425
So those contains all the logs
that [INAUDIBLE] by Airflow.

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:33.370
I want to say that those
logs are very helpful.

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:35.970
Sometimes when you
have a task failure,

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:37.620
you have no idea
what's happening.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:42.420
Those logs provides the
initial place for you

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:44.910
to do investigation.

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:48.120
A lot of times, the failures or
exceptions will be logged here.

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:57.010
There are other views for
you to manage your task.

00:29:57.010 --> 00:29:59.990
They provide task views,
which you see tasks

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:04.960
from all workflows running
in this environment.

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:07.720
You would also be able to
see the active DAG runs.

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:11.860
So you have DAG
view, task view--

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:12.870
depends on your need.

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:14.830
They're very flexible
for you to navigate

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:16.780
through your workflows.

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:19.510
Sometimes you can also
create a manual run in case,

00:30:19.510 --> 00:30:23.950
for example, if you
don't want to wait

00:30:23.950 --> 00:30:26.800
until the scheduled interval,
it is also possible for you

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:30.790
to just create a
manual run yourself.

00:30:30.790 --> 00:30:36.580
Just put a DAG ID, give it a
run ID, and then click Save.

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:41.514
That it will start a manual
run of the same workflow.

00:30:41.514 --> 00:30:43.180
Now we have seen that
all the tasks have

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:44.870
been successfully completed.

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:50.060
And then you also have the
[? summary ?] [? tasks ?] here

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:52.360
as well, like how long does
it for a task to complete?

00:30:56.430 --> 00:31:00.660
So that that's the
end of the demo.

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:02.480
We're going to switch
back to the slides.

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:22.870
So here's a quick
recap on the demo.

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:27.060
We just illustrated, we
just showed you how to--

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:29.476
we skipped the part of creating
a Composer environment.

00:31:29.476 --> 00:31:30.350
There's a reason why.

00:31:30.350 --> 00:31:35.360
I'm going to discuss
that and also

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:37.260
give you different
ways-- in this case,

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:39.860
we primarily focus on
Cloud Console for you

00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:41.800
to access the
Composer environment.

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:44.510
But it is also
possible to interact

00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:50.840
with Composer environment using
Cloud SDK or even the API.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:53.750
Next, we're showing you
how to upload a DAG--

00:31:53.750 --> 00:31:56.710
as simple as just drag and
drop a file into that GCS

00:31:56.710 --> 00:31:58.100
[INAUDIBLE].

00:31:58.100 --> 00:32:01.040
And then we have walked
you through the various UI

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:05.490
features comes together with
Apache Airflow web server.

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:07.190
And finally, we also
talked about why

00:32:07.190 --> 00:32:11.000
we want to use DAG drivers
to exam Composer logs.

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.010
So you don't have to
wait until a task is

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:14.510
done so you can
see streaming logs

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:16.850
coming in as the task executes.

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:25.180
I'm going to wrap up the webinar
by giving you some pointers

00:32:25.180 --> 00:32:26.020
to get you started.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:33.010
Given Composer is a manager
of Apache Airflow service,

00:32:33.010 --> 00:32:35.040
so all Apache
Airflow [? confabs ?]

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.560
would apply in Composer.

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:41.550
We recommend to you to go
over the Apache Airflow

00:32:41.550 --> 00:32:45.510
documentations and also some
of the Start Guide there.

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:47.760
They have very good
documentation explaining

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:49.920
the concepts of DAGs.

00:32:49.920 --> 00:32:53.950
They also give you a lot
of tutorials as well.

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:57.984
Specifically, the Cloud
Composer documentation website

00:32:57.984 --> 00:32:59.400
will also give you
all the details

00:32:59.400 --> 00:33:03.600
about how you interact with
the Composer environment,

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:05.080
how do you create
an environment,

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:06.538
how do you manage
your environment,

00:33:06.538 --> 00:33:09.310
and how would you
access your environment?

00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:12.210
In addition, it will also give
you price information how would

00:33:12.210 --> 00:33:13.890
you monitor your
Composer environment,

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:17.520
how to interact with Stackdriver
overflows-- excuse me--

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:21.430
how to interact with Stackdriver
Logging and monitoring.

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:26.250
And finally, the team is
trying to add more DAG examples

00:33:26.250 --> 00:33:27.300
in Composer.

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:31.320
So we have a few
examples in the GitHub.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:34.280
but the team plans
to add more there.

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:38.090
So that will be the end
of the presentation.

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:42.275
Stay tuned for the live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in a few minutes.

00:34:21.970 --> 00:34:25.620
Welcome back to the Q&amp;A session.

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:28.575
So I'm going to read out
the questions posted here.

00:34:28.575 --> 00:34:30.199
So the first question
is about how long

00:34:30.199 --> 00:34:34.100
does it take to create
my Composer environment?

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:37.039
We have received some customer
feedback saying, hey, seems

00:34:37.039 --> 00:34:40.370
to take a long time for me to
create my Composer environment.

00:34:40.370 --> 00:34:44.909
Sometimes it can be
15 or even 20 minutes.

00:34:44.909 --> 00:34:50.120
The reason here is that
together with Composer, we also

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:54.380
host the Airflow web server
inside a Google App Engine.

00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:56.480
And for those of you
who have used Google App

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:58.340
Engine in the past,
you probably know

00:34:58.340 --> 00:35:01.730
that it takes about 15
minutes to get your App Engine

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:04.610
app created, initialized,
and stabilized.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:07.760
So that's the reason
why creating a composing

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:12.480
environment takes
15 to 20 minutes.

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:15.030
Now the second
question is about,

00:35:15.030 --> 00:35:16.700
how do I get to
email notifications

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:19.640
when the workflow fails?

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:24.380
If you still recall, when
we worked through the DAG

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:26.540
structure, in one
section, there's

00:35:26.540 --> 00:35:29.450
a configuration option in
default [INAUDIBLE] section,

00:35:29.450 --> 00:35:31.820
where you can say,
hey, email on failure

00:35:31.820 --> 00:35:34.520
or receive email notification.

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:37.760
By the way, you also need to
put an email address there.

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:41.540
So given there some limitations
in Google Cloud platform,

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:45.150
mainly from spam and a security
perspective, that you can use

00:35:45.150 --> 00:35:49.610
SMTP directly when running
your application in GKE.

00:35:49.610 --> 00:35:57.580
So instead, in this case,
we use [? centigrade ?]

00:35:57.580 --> 00:36:00.160
for all that email
notifications.

00:36:00.160 --> 00:36:06.160
To config email for using
[? centigrade ?] service,

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:10.870
really, you only need to supply
an email in centigrade API key

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:13.120
as well as a form address.

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:16.000
And then those can be configured
as part of your Composer

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:17.970
environment variables.

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:20.260
Our documentation website
has a lot more details

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:23.170
about what kind of
environment variables

00:36:23.170 --> 00:36:25.690
should you put into your
Composer environment

00:36:25.690 --> 00:36:29.600
to get an email working for you.

00:36:29.600 --> 00:36:33.380
The next question is about
which Apache Airflow versions

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:35.214
does Composer support?

00:36:35.214 --> 00:36:36.380
That's a very good question.

00:36:36.380 --> 00:36:41.860
So right now, Composer
only supports Airflow 1.9.

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:45.190
It's on a roadmap to support
multiple versions of Apache

00:36:45.190 --> 00:36:46.130
Airflow.

00:36:46.130 --> 00:36:48.910
So for example, at
environment creation time,

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:53.720
you could specify, hey, I want
to use Airflow 1.8 or Airflow

00:36:53.720 --> 00:36:54.610
1.10.

00:36:54.610 --> 00:36:59.410
So those multi-version support
is on our roadmap for sure.

00:36:59.410 --> 00:37:04.520
But right now, only
1.9 is supported.

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:07.010
The next question
is, do you have

00:37:07.010 --> 00:37:09.680
any suggestion on
migrating on-prem Airflow

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:12.430
setup to Composer?

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:19.040
So ideally, given Composer as a
manage Apache Airflow solution,

00:37:19.040 --> 00:37:22.270
your DAG should work
transparently across on-prem

00:37:22.270 --> 00:37:24.350
and GCP and vice versa.

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:27.360
Sometimes we do notice
that if your DAGs has

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:29.820
a reference to the local
environment-- for example,

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:35.360
if you try to refer to
local [? fault ?] systems

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:42.400
or other nonstandard
Airflow operators,

00:37:42.400 --> 00:37:45.510
those might be a
friction for you.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:47.770
So you want to make sure
those got corrected.

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:52.180
And then if you make sure that
your workflow doesn't refer

00:37:52.180 --> 00:37:54.680
to anything in your
local environment,

00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:58.730
then in addition of copying
your DAGs from your on-prem

00:37:58.730 --> 00:38:01.540
to Composer, the other
thing you need to concern

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:04.520
will be the outflow
database migration.

00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:07.940
If it's the same
Airflow version,

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:15.290
the migration can be as easy
as export your database data

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:19.310
and import that
again in Composer.

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:24.140
Or if you don't want to
retain all the history,

00:38:24.140 --> 00:38:26.270
you could also
selectively just copy over

00:38:26.270 --> 00:38:30.530
the connections or variable
configurations you need.

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:35.580
Next question is,
can I orchestrate

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:37.680
at a hybrid environment
with Composer?

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:40.080
Yes, that's totally possible.

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.150
So you could have task
running on [? prem ?]

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:47.220
as long as you make sure
that, for example, you

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:51.420
have the right network
connection set up in place.

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:54.650
Composer or Airflow is really
agnostic to the type of task

00:38:54.650 --> 00:38:57.230
that gets executed.

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:59.650
The next question
is very similar.

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:02.280
Can I orchestrate a multi-core
environment with Composer.

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:08.940
Yes-- as I mentioned
earlier, they are connectors

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:14.040
not only to GCP, but there are
also connectors to AWS or Agile

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:18.570
or other cloud providers
in Apache Airflow.

00:39:18.570 --> 00:39:22.110
So really, the only
thing you need to do here

00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:26.150
is to make sure you have
the credentials config

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:30.030
as connections in
Airflow or Composer.

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:33.720
And afterwards, when you try
to instantiate an operator

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:35.940
to a non-cloud
provider, make sure you

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:38.310
refer to the right
connection name string.

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:42.145
So that's all you need to do.

00:39:42.145 --> 00:39:44.560
The last question
is about, oh, I

00:39:44.560 --> 00:39:46.360
have a lot of tasks
in my workflow.

00:39:46.360 --> 00:39:50.050
How does a composer handle
Airflow worker scaling?

00:39:50.050 --> 00:39:57.340
Very good question-- for
now, the composer API service

00:39:57.340 --> 00:40:01.400
does allow you to manually
scale the number of workers.

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:05.540
So for example, if you
realize that, oh, suddenly you

00:40:05.540 --> 00:40:10.200
have a burst of tasks where
you need to [? stand ?]

00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:14.830
maybe 1,000 tasks or
machine is running

00:40:14.830 --> 00:40:16.420
short of machine resource.

00:40:16.420 --> 00:40:20.700
What do you need to do is just
log in to the Cloud Console

00:40:20.700 --> 00:40:23.635
where [? your ?] [? class ?]
[? SDK ?] to upsize the number

00:40:23.635 --> 00:40:25.610
of worker nodes you have.

00:40:25.610 --> 00:40:31.510
It will automatically
manage the scaling for you,

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:34.810
as well as making sure that
Airflow configurations are

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:38.530
updated accordingly to
match your cluster size.

00:40:38.530 --> 00:40:42.670
Notably, there are
a few scalability

00:40:42.670 --> 00:40:45.330
or parallelism
configuration options

00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:47.330
related to the number of
workers you could have.

00:40:47.330 --> 00:40:50.986
So those will be taken
care of by Composer.

00:40:50.986 --> 00:40:54.670
So you don't have to
worry about that at all.

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:57.940
In addition, auto scaling is
also on our official roadmap.

00:40:57.940 --> 00:41:01.520
So we may have auto
scaling support sometime.

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:04.750
As a result, you don't have
to worry about scaling at all.

00:41:04.750 --> 00:41:10.390
So that will be the
end of the Q&amp;A session.

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:15.020
Thanks a lot for staying
to listen to the webinar

00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:19.020
and definitely stay in tune
for next Tuesday's webinars.

